# XSH getlogin(), getlogin_r()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# What the login name is depends on the login activity that started the test
# run, which the suite cannot arrange; many environments (batch, container,
# no controlling terminal) have none. The page lets getlogin() return a
# null pointer then, and allows getlogin_r() to fail. The tests therefore
# check what holds in every case: the form of what is returned, agreement
# between the two functions and between repeated calls, ERANGE when a name
# exists, and the thread-safety of getlogin_r().

assertion  XSH.getlogin.001
anchor     functions/getlogin.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/getlogin.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       getlogin() returns either a null pointer or a pointer to a
           non-empty null-terminated string no longer than {LOGIN_NAME_MAX}
           (when sysconf(_SC_LOGIN_NAME_MAX) reports a limit), both in the
           test process and in a child that has called setsid() (no
           controlling terminal).
note       A null pointer is a valid result in both (the name cannot be
           found); errno is not looked at.
test       t/001-form.c

assertion  XSH.getlogin.002
anchor     functions/getlogin.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       Repeated calls to getlogin() agree: either all return null or
           all return the same name.
test       t/002-stable.c

assertion  XSH.getlogin.003
anchor     functions/getlogin.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       getlogin_r() returns 0, or a positive error number (the value is
           returned, not -1); when it returns 0 the buffer holds a
           non-empty null-terminated name that fits in the namesize
           given.
test       t/003-getlogin-r-form.c

assertion  XSH.getlogin.004
anchor     functions/getlogin.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       When getlogin() and getlogin_r() both succeed in the same
           process, they give the same name.
note       If either does not succeed here, the test is UNRESOLVED (there
           is nothing to compare).
test       t/004-agree.c

assertion  XSH.getlogin.005
anchor     functions/getlogin.html#ERRORS/ERANGE
class      required
since      2024
says       When getlogin_r() succeeds with a large buffer and the name is n
           bytes, a call with namesize n (one short of the name and its
           null byte) returns ERANGE, and a call with namesize n+1
           succeeds.
note       Needs an environment with a login name; UNRESOLVED otherwise.
           ERANGE became a shall-fail in Issue 8 (defect 398).
test       t/005-erange.c

assertion  XSH.getlogin.006
anchor     functions/getlogin.html#APPLICATION USAGE
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.1
class      required
since      2008
says       getlogin_r() is thread-safe: four threads that call it 500 times
           each into buffers of their own get the same results as one
           call in the main thread (same return value and, on success,
           the same name).
test       t/006-threads.c
